    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This dissertation describes a technique for mathematically simulating the solution-gas drive performance of a volatile oil reservoir on a digital computer. In the mathematical model, it is assumed that the phase behavior of the reservoir fluids may be approximated by a ternary hydrocarbon system and that the reservoir fluids are always in a state of thermodynamic equilibrium. Consideration was given to pressure distributions, fluid-saturation variations, and composition distributions as they occur in the oil reservoir during production. The mathematical properties of the fundamental flow equations for a ternary system of two distinct phases allowed the development of a numerical method to determine pressure, composition, and fluid-saturation distributions in transient flow systems.The relationships between the overall concentration of the individual hydrocarbon components and the characteristics of the gas-oil relative permeability curves are shown quantitatively These indicate that the gas liberation process may change appreciably with respect to time and space during the production history of a volatile oil reservoir. The production rate has a negligible effect upon the recovery of less volatile hydrocarbon components from a volatile oil reservoir, but the nature of the relative permeability curves does influence appreciably the estimated recovery from such oil reservoirs.Approximately ten minutes of computer time (Burroughs 5500) per run are required for the constant production-rate calculations reported. The reservoir studied was a linear system but extension to a radial system is outlined. The analysis developed may also be extended to hydrocarbon systems with more than three components.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
